## Dataset setup

1. Download `Audio_Speech_Actors_01-24_16k.zip` from [RAVDESS Speech 16K on Zenodo](https://zenodo.org/records/11063852).
2. Create a `dataset` folder next to `main.cpp` and extract the archive into it. A recording should be at a path like `dataset/Audio_Speech_Actors_01-24_16k/Actor_17/03-01-01-01-01-01-17.wav`.

## Build and run

Open PowerShell in the project folder. You need `g++` with C++17 support. The program uses the C++ standard library.

```powershell
g++ -std=c++17 -O2 main.cpp -o voice.exe
.\voice.exe
```

The program trains and tests automatically when it runs. At the last prompt, enter the path to a 16 kHz, 16-bit PCM WAV file to classify it, or press Enter to exit.

## How it works

1. Estimate pitch using autocorrelation and count zero crossings in each recording. Scale both values before using them in the model.
2. Fit the regression model `a * x1 + b * x2 + c` on actors 01–16. Use central finite differences to estimate the mean squared error gradient and gradient descent to update `a`, `b`, and `c`.
3. Test on actors 17–24. For each recording, a score of at least 0.5 predicts the female label. A lower score predicts the male label.

## Example results

| Metric | Result |
|---|---:|
| Training clips used | 914 of 960 |
| Test clips classified | 458 of 480 |
| Training mean squared error | 0.262175 → 0.173580 |
| Test accuracy on classified clips | 67.90% (311 of 458) |

Recordings without a usable pitch estimate are skipped. The program reports how many it skipped in each split.
